%I A399501 #32 Oct 15 2026 18:48:17
%S A399501 0,0,0,0,0,0,2,38,158,598,1884,3834,6230,9026,12770,17460,23310,30494,
%T A399501 39198
%N A399501 Number of ways to place 2 pawns on an n X n board such that n+2 nonattacking queens can be placed on the remaining squares.
%C A399501 The pawns are blockers only: queens do not attack pawns and pawns do not attack queens. A pawn interrupts a line of attack, so two queens on the same row, column or diagonal do not attack each other if a pawn lies between them (the convention of A129551).
%C A399501 This is the k = 2 case of the n+k queens problem (Chatham et al.): with k pawns placed as blockers, up to n+k nonattacking queens fit on an n X n board. Cf. A103330 (k = 1) and A129553 (k = 3).
%C A399501 A129551(n) is the total number of (pawn placement, queen placement) configurations; a(n) counts the pawn placements that admit at least one queen placement. a(n) <= binomial(n^2,2).
%C A399501 a(n) = 0 for n <= 6 because n+2 nonattacking queens cannot be placed on an n X n board with 2 pawns for n <= 6 (A129551(n) = 0).
%H A399501 R. D. Chatham, <a href="https://www.npluskqueens.info/">The N+k Queens Problem Page</a>.
%H A399501 R. D. Chatham, M. Doyle, G. H. Fricke, J. Reitmann, R. D. Skaggs and M. Wolff, <a href="https://www.npluskqueens.info/uploads/2/1/3/5/21355572/queenssep2.pdf">Independence and Domination Separation on Chessboard Graphs</a>, J. Combin. Math. Combin. Comput. 68 (2009), 3-17.
%e A399501 For n = 7 the two pawn placements are {(2,2),(4,4)} and its image under the anti-diagonal reflection (0-based (row,col) coordinates); each admits exactly 2 placements of the 9 queens, giving A129551(7) = 4.
%Y A399501 Cf. A129551, A129552, A103330, A129553.
%K A399501 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A399501 1,7
%A A399501 _Jonathan Carroll_, Oct 02 2026
